  "generated_text": "1. The equation of the parabola is \\( y = x^2 \\).\n2. The slope of the tangent at point \\( A = (1,1) \\) is given by the derivative of \\( y = x^2 \\), which is \\( 2x \\).\n3. At point \\( A \\), the slope of the tangent is \\( 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\).\n4. The slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent, which is \\( -\\frac{1}{2} \\).\n5. The equation of the normal line passing through point \\( A \\) is \\( y - 1 = -\\frac{1}{2}(x - 1) \\).\n6. Simplifying this equation, we get \\( y = -\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{3}{2} \\).\n7. To find the coordinates of point \\( B \\), we need to solve the system of equations \\( y = x^2 \\) and \\( y = -\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{3}{2} \\).\n\n\\boxed{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}, \\frac{9}{4}\\right)}",
  "grade": {
